 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Towns and the countryside depend on each other.\nWhat the countryside gives towns:\n• food (plantain, cassava, vegetables, meat, fish) to feed townspeople;\n• raw materials for factories (cocoa, coffee, cotton, timber, rubber, palm oil);\n• labour (workers who move to towns).\nWhat towns give the countryside:\n• manufactured goods (tools, cloth, soap, phones), fertilisers and medicines;\n• markets where farmers sell their crops;\n• services: banks, hospitals, secondary schools, administration;\n• money sent home by family members working in towns (remittances).\nRoads and markets link them: bad roads cause food to rot and prices to rise in town.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "How do bad farm-to-market roads affect both the farmer and the townsperson?",
  "steps": [
   "The farmer cannot take crops to town quickly, so some rot and he earns less.",
   "Less food reaches town, so prices in town go up."
  ],
  "answer": "The farmer loses crops and income; townspeople pay higher prices.",
  "checks": []
 },
